/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This layered importance structure affirms the value of a hybrid system where lexical specificity is used to narrow down candidates, and semantic understanding is used to refine the final results. Such a design not only improves performance but also supports explainability, which is especially valuable in sensitive domains like legal or academic search systems.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (6. Conclusion )
/E (6. Conclusion )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This project set out to develop a hybrid document retrieval system that combines the speed of TF-IDF-based lexical filtering with the semantic depth of BERT embeddings. By integrating these two powerful techniques, we successfully addressed the limitations of traditional keyword search while avoiding the high computational cost of deep learning applied at scale.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Our methodology involved thorough data preprocessing, feature engineering, and vector representation, followed by the implementation of a two-stage retrieval pipeline. TF-IDF served as an efficient first-pass filter to reduce the document pool, while BERT was used to semantically re-rank the top candidates. This hybrid approach allowed us to capture both explicit keyword matches and contextual relevance, providing users with more meaningful and accurate search results.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In summary, the hybrid search engine presented in this project offers a scalable, intelligent, and practical solution for modern document discovery tasks. Its flexibility makes it well-suited for applications in legal research, academic indexing, corporate knowledge systems, and more.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
